A||||7||BroCar 87||Amplifier with only RCA cable hook ups... help!||||4||
Z||000000||BroCar 87||12-03-2000||11:22 PM||||O.K. guys, I have an amp that only can be hooked up with RCA cables. I went out and bought some right angle solderless RCA plugs. I was told that you can hook up speaker wires to the RCA plug and plug that into the amp. As I opened up the plastic covering, I see that there is only 1 metal prong where the wires are supposed to be. BUT there is also a little screw in the center of the RCA plug. Am I supposed to wire the + to the prong, and the - to the screw? Or did the salesman give me the wrong thing?<BR>Any info would be appreciated!<BR>Thanks<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Signature2.jpg">  <BR>87 Coupe "IRON BEAST"<BR>   Custom dual side exit exhaust!||||reg||4
Z||000001||RNGCRSH||12-03-2000||11:26 PM||||Post a picture or E-mail me. picture says a thousand words.<P>is this for the input of the amp? if so it probably cant handle being driven by speaker outs from a radio. <p>[This message has been edited by RNGCRSH (edited 12-03-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Oreif||12-03-2000||11:32 PM||||The center of the RCA is +<BR>The outside ring is -<BR>Whoever told you you could hook up high outputs to an amplifiers low level inputs was wrong. The circuitry in the amp will amplify all noise and then it will screw up the impedence of the input, feedback onto itself, and finally kill the amplifier.||||reg||1
Z||000003||2xsess||12-04-2000||06:11 AM||||Go to Radio Shack and buy a converter, I bought on for $25 last year. It's a little box with wires on on side and the RCA outputs on the other side. It even had level adjustments.||||reg||1
Z||000004||william_thorniii||12-04-2000||08:35 AM||||Brocar,<P>Can this be done?  Yes.<P>Should it be done?  No.  <P>Some amplifiers do have a high-level input.  These are simple connections where one can attach speaker wires to the amplifier from the head unit.  I understood that when these were used, the amplifier would then alter the signal to clean it up before "re-amplifying".  <P>Most high quality amplifiers use RCA's from the head unit to the amplifier.  I would reccomend using this convention.  If it means that you must purchase another head unit with RCA outputs so be it.  A new head unit can be had much more inexpensively than a new amplifier.  <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||1
Z||000005||BroCar 87||12-04-2000||12:46 PM||||O.K. Thanks guys, I don't wanna mess up my amp, so I am going to wait until I get a "real" head unit. Not this factory P.O.S.<BR>Thanks again people!<BR><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Signature2.jpg">  <BR>87 Coupe "IRON BEAST"<BR>   Custom dual side exit exhaust!||||reg||10
Z||000006||batboy||12-04-2000||09:32 PM||||Yes it can be done. You have to use a line converter like 2xsess says, except don't buy that Radio Shack crap. Call Crutchfield's or another reputable stereo shop and get a good one. They'll help you get the right stuff.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Tom88gt||12-04-2000||09:37 PM||||You need an amp which accepts speaker level inputs.  Or the converter that the others mentioned.  Either will work but the problem is distortion.  Low level inputs are really the only way to go to minimize distortion.  Get a new head unit with pre-amp outputs/RCA plugs.||||reg||1
